WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.376
Being apart of 'Hidden Figures'

00:00:01.376 --> 00:00:02.210
was the very first time

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.087
I was a part of an
Oscar nominated film.

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.338
You will never forget

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.007
that first moment
in your career.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:14.681
I was a real Oscar
obsessed kid,

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:18.309
so it's hard for me to
choose a moment.

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:20.854
When Anna Paquin
won for 'The Piano',

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.480
because I was the same
age as her.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.107
And I really wanted
to be an actress.

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:25.650
I was so jealous of her.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.236
The first thing that came to mind
was Roberto Benigni.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:29.154
Me too.

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:30.196
Roberto Benigni.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.865
When he won the Oscar for
'Life is Beautiful'

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:33.450
and he’s jumping over
the seats.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.452
Sam Rockwell winning
for 'Three Billboards'.

00:00:35.452 --> 00:00:37.454
Early 1990s when
Jack Palance did

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:40.123
one handed push ups at
age 70-something.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:43.043
Stanley Donen was the
director of the show

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:45.211
and he insisted that
the three

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:48.798
gentlemen gave the Best
Picture Oscar would be

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:52.177
John Huston, Billy Wilder,
and Akira Kurosawa.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:55.597
You could feel the warmth
and self-respect

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:57.432
for the movie business
in that moment.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:01:00.393
An Oscar moment that
speaks to me

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:02.604
was Sally Field's
acceptance speech.

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:05.148
I can't deny the fact
that you like me.

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.150
My former television mother,
Sally Field.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:08.485
“They like me, 
they really like me.”

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:10.820
Madonna singing "Sooner or Later" from 'Dick Tracy'

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.529
on the Oscars

00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:13.490
is probably the best
musical performance

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.866
on the Oscars ever.

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:17.494
The moment when
'The Times of Harvey Milk' won.

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:19.412
It made me realize that
I did want to

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.371
make documentaries.

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.706
When Whoopi Goldberg
won her Oscar.

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:23.041
It was a very memorable
moment for me.

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.793
When Whoopi Goldberg
won for 'Ghost',

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.836
and closed the gap of
so many years

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:29.047
without a black actor
being on that stage.

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:31.466
When Halle Berry won
Best Actress,

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:32.550
and that was the first time

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.344
that a woman of
color had won.

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:35.345
It was quite significant.

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:40.183
This moment is
so much bigger than me.

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:41.768
I have many memories

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.354
having grown up
on the Oscars,

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:45.939
but it was pretty extraordinary

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:47.982
to get to have my birthday

00:01:47.982 --> 00:01:50.360
at the Oscars and
win the Oscar,

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.278
for Noah Baumbach's
'Marriage Story'.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.072
The streaker stands out to me.

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:55.907
One of those things,
was it planned?

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:57.242
Was it spontaneous?

00:01:57.242 --> 00:01:58.827
We don't know.
Who cares?

00:01:58.827 --> 00:01:59.869
That stays in my mind.

